# Shell scripts run on the Linux VPS, authored on Windows dev machines --
# without this, git's default autocrlf behavior on Windows can commit them
# with CRLF line endings, which typically breaks bash outright (a CRLF
# shebang line commonly fails as "bad interpreter: ...bash^M: no such file
# or directory"). Found live 2026-08-15: the already-committed
# db/checkpoint-and-publish.sh has CRLF endings in this repo right now --
# if that script is actually running via cron on the VPS today, it was
# presumably normalized to LF there out-of-band (e.g. dos2unix, or a editor
# save) rather than by anything git itself enforced. This makes LF the
# enforced, repo-level default going forward instead of relying on that
# happening by hand every time.
*.sh text eol=lf
# Same reasoning applies to the Safariis backup templates (db/backup/) --
# they're real shell scripts meant to be copied onto a Linux box and have
# their .template suffix dropped, just not matched by the *.sh rule above
# because of that suffix. Found while committing them 2026-09-19 (git
# warned about CRLF on these specifically, not on the sibling *.sh files --
# this rule already covered those).
*.sh.template text eol=lf
# systemd unit files -- less certain to break on a stray \r than bash's
# shebang line is, but not worth finding out live on the VPS either.
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer text eol=lf
